WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.850
My agent did the first

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:02.930
two steps perfectly.

3
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.950
Then I replied yes, and it

4
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.270
greeted me like a stranger!

5
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.020
Nothing was broken.

6
00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.430
My question had just fallen

7
00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:12.250
out of its memory.

8
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.400
Today: how agents plan, and

9
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.620
what fits in their memory.

10
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.380
Agents plan in two common ways.

11
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.190
Plan first: write every

12
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.710
step, then run them.

13
00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.790
Or step by step: look at each

14
00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.910
result, then choose the next step.

15
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.080
Darsi Tiny writes a one-line plan,

16
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.240
then calls its tools in turn.

17
00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.130
Each step builds on the last.

18
00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.610
On Saturday, the timetable

19
00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.330
tool answers: no school.

20
00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.010
Darsi Tiny sets the reminder

21
00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.830
anyway: six B, six B.

22
00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.450
A failed step that nobody checks

23
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.470
becomes the next step’s input.

24
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.210
An agent’s only memory is its context

25
00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.030
window: every token the model reads at

26
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:00.290
once.

27
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.680
It holds the tools, the

28
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.080
conversation so far, and room to write.

29
00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.150
Darsi Tiny’s holds three hundred and

30
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.050
eighty-four tokens; big cloud models,

31
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.890
about a hundred and twenty-eight thousand.

32
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.970
When the window fills, the

33
00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.430
app chooses what stays.

34
00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.770
Keep everything, and the

35
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.010
fourth answer is cut off.

36
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.270
Drop the oldest, and the

37
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.810
question behind a yes falls out.

38
00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.390
Keep short notes, and all

39
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.630
seven messages work.

40
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.480
Quick check!

41
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.310
The app drops the oldest turns.

42
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.450
I ask for tomorrow’s weather, it asks

43
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.730
which village, and I say Dala.

44
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.000
It gives me today’s weather.

45
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:47.140
Why?

46
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.150
Because tomorrow fell out of the window.

47
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.740
The model never saw it, so

48
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:54.820
it used a default.

49
00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.890
It can’t tell you what it has forgotten.

50
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.740
Only the app knows what it dropped.

51
00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.550
So engineers decide what stays.

52
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.360
Keep recent turns, and turn

53
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.820
older ones into notes.

54
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.820
Pin the facts the task depends on,

55
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:13.620
like the day and the place.

56
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.780
Or fetch from documents only the part

57
00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.900
a step needs: that’s retrieval.

58
00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.220
In this lesson’s code,

59
00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.720
you’ll write the trimming.

60
00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:24.810
Walk back from the newest

61
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.730
turn, adding up tokens.

62
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.500
Stop at the first turn that won’t fit,

63
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.460
so the conversation has no gaps.

64
00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.310
In the app, send seven messages

65
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.990
and watch the window fill.

66
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.960
Try Keep everything, then Drop the

67
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.200
oldest at a hundred and twenty-eight

68
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.200
tokens, then Keep notes.

69
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.500
See which turn falls out.

70
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.240
Your turn.

71
00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.610
An agent helps plan a three-day

72
00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.530
field trip over a long chat.

73
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.400
Which memory strategy is safest?

74
00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.150
Notes, with the dates and places pinned.

75
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.500
Remember: check each

76
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.300
step’s result as you go.

77
00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.770
The context window is the

78
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.950
model’s only memory.

79
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.380
And the app, not the model,

80
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:12.660
decides what stays.

81
00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.510
Try it yourself: picture an agent that

82
00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.150
helps your group study for a week.

83
00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.530
Write the two-line note you would

84
00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.070
pin at the top of its window: who,

85
00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.570
what, when and where.

86
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.950
Then decide what it may drop.
